# kernel-csky.img      -- Linux 6.5.10 plus two patches (csky gcc 12.3.0)
# kernel-csky-7.3.img  -- Linux 7.3    plus the same two and a third one
#                         (csky gcc 15.2.0)
#
# None of the patches is upstream yet.
#
# 1. "csky: add exception table entries for the cmpxchg trap"
#
# On CPUs without ldex/stex (the ck810 here) every user-space atomic
# goes through trap 2, csky_cmpxchg, which loads and stores the user word in
# kernel mode without an exception table entry.  A fault there that the page
# fault handler cannot resolve -- a fatal signal pending, or an unmapped
# address -- oopses the kernel ("Unable to handle kernel paging request",
# pc in csky_cmpxchg_ldw).  uClibc-ng's pthread/cancellation-points hits it
# in its test of system(): the child faults on copy-on-write pages while the
# cancelled parent's cleanup SIGKILLs it.
#
# 2. "csky: mm: make PROT_NONE ptes present and drop stale hardware bits"
#
# csky's PAGE_NONE was a software marker without _PAGE_PRESENT, so the core
# mm took a PROT_NONE pte for a swap entry ("BUG: Bad rss-counter state ...
# MM_SWAPENTS"), and pte_modify() kept the hardware _PAGE_VALID/_PAGE_DIRTY
# bits of the old protection.  A page that had been written and was then
# made PROT_NONE stayed writable for the kernel: semctl(IPC_STAT) and read()
# into it succeeded instead of failing with EFAULT, and tst-ipc-ctl-buffers
# failed.  The fix makes PAGE_NONE present without any access rights, as
# mips does, and recomputes the hardware bits in pte_modify().
#
# 3. kernel-csky-7.3.img only: "csky: irq_work raises an interrupt only on SMP"
#
# csky's arch_irq_work_has_interrupt() returns true even on uniprocessor kernels, where arch_irq_work_raise() is
# the empty default, so a queued irq_work never runs.  Since v7.0-rc6
# (a6fc88b22bc8, "srcu: Use irq_work to start GP in tiny SRCU") Tiny SRCU
# starts every grace period that way, and synchronize_srcu() never returns:
# close() of an inotify fd hung and tst-inotify timed out.  Found by
# bisecting v6.5..v7.3.  6.5.10 does not need it.
#
# Drop each patch once a release has it.
#
# Why two kernels: v6.11 changed csky's syscall 84 from sync_file_range to
# sync_file_range2, which takes the flags before the offsets (kernel commit
# 3339b99ef6fe, "csky, hexagon: fix broken sys_sync_file_range").  A uClibc-ng
# built against older headers calls it the old way, and a 7.x kernel answers
# EINVAL.  So kernel-csky.img stays at 6.5.10 for the target built against
# 5.4.259 headers, and kernel-csky-7.3.img goes with the target built against
# 7.3 headers.  v7.3 also has no csky vDSO any more.
#
# kernel-csky.img is the 6.5.10 image as before, rebuilt with the patches:
# same config, same compiler.
#
# Build:
#
#   <linux> = linux-6.5.10 or v7.3, with the patches applied
#   cp kernel-csky.config (or kernel-csky-7.3.config) <obj>/.config
#   make -C <linux> O=<obj> ARCH=csky CROSS_COMPILE=csky-openadk-linux-uclibcabiv2- olddefconfig
#   make -C <linux> O=<obj> ARCH=csky CROSS_COMPILE=csky-openadk-linux-uclibcabiv2-
#   csky-openadk-linux-uclibcabiv2-strip -o kernel-csky.img <obj>/vmlinux
#
# The rootfs is not passed with -initrd: CONFIG_INITRAMFS_SOURCE points at
# ../kernel-xtensa.cpio, the 17 MiB placeholder that uc_devel and the CI
# overwrite with the real rootfs.  It must lie next to <obj>.
